{
  "title": "Implement held-out matched-word rightwardness by line length and section",
  "program": "L2",
  "on_path": false,
  "hard": true,
  "statement": "On public practice-training pages, matched sh/ch, qo/o and k/t word sets show the reported pooled mid-line direction in both ZL and IT; their section and length strata vary, and some reverse. These exploratory estimates do not establish differences between strata. The measurement language cannot select line-length strata, construct matched word sets, or compare their mean relative positions. The requested held-out comparisons therefore cannot yet be registered as facts.",
  "check": "Add a reviewed scorer for prespecified matched-word rightwardness contrasts with explicit matching and ambiguity rules, line-length and section strata, minimum sample sizes, and uncertainty accounting for page clustering. Validate on synthetic known-effects and null data, then preregister thresholds and evaluate on hidden pages with independent IT replication; do not select strata or thresholds from this exploratory output.",
  "method": "Paragraph lines with at least three tokens, excluding line edges and uncertain tokens. Relative position is zero-based index divided by line token count minus one, using the original line length. Pair eligibility is determined from certain interior tokens on the public training pages independently for each transcription: sh vs ch prefixes, qo vs o prefixes, and a single k/t character substitution anywhere. A token is counted once per contrast even if it has multiple k/t counterparts. The two sides are pooled, not equally weighted by matched type. Length bins 3–7, 8–11, and 12+ use all original line tokens. Results require 20 tokens per side, without statistical significance or multiplicity claims. Different from Feaster: modern data, certain tokens only, no ambiguity resolution, practice-training selection and this explicit matching rule.",
  "source": "Patrick Feaster, Rightward and Downward Grapheme Distributions in the Voynich Manuscript (2022), https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-3313/paper12.pdf",
